28. Naive Forecasting

The simplest forecasting method:

Assume the next value will be the same as the previous value.

For example:

Monday = 100
Tuesday = 120
Wednesday = 130

Prediction for Thursday:

130

It sounds simple, but it provides a useful baseline.

29. Seasonal Naive Forecasting

If your data has seasonality, you can use the value from the previous season.

For example, with weekly seasonality:

Today's prediction = value from the same day last week.

If:

Monday last week = 100

then:

Monday this week prediction = 100

This is another useful baseline.

30. Moving Average Forecast

A moving average predicts using the average of recent observations.

For example:

Last 3 sales:

100
120
140

Prediction:

(100 + 120 + 140) / 3 = 120
31. Exponential Smoothing

Exponential smoothing gives more importance to recent observations.

The general idea is:

Recent data → more weight
Older data → less weight

This is useful when recent observations are more informative about the future.

32. AR Model

AR = Autoregressive

An AR model predicts the current value using previous values.

For example:

Today's sales =
    coefficient × yesterday's sales
    + error

An AR(p) model uses the previous p observations.

For example:

AR(1)

uses one lag.

AR(5)

uses five lags.

33. MA Model

MA = Moving Average model

Despite the name, this is different from simply calculating a moving average.

An MA model uses previous forecast errors to help predict the current value.

An MA(q) model uses the previous q error terms.

34. ARMA

ARMA combines:

AR + MA

So it uses:

previous observations
previous errors

It is generally applied to stationary series.

35. ARIMA

ARIMA = AutoRegressive Integrated Moving Average

It has three parameters:

ARIMA(p, d, q)
p → AR order

Number of previous values used.

d → Differencing

Number of times the data is differenced to achieve stationarity.

q → MA order

Number of previous error terms used.

Example:

ARIMA(2,1,1)

means:

2 AR terms
first-order differencing
1 MA term
36. SARIMA

SARIMA = Seasonal ARIMA

Used when the data has seasonality.

It extends ARIMA with seasonal components.

Often represented as:

SARIMA(p,d,q)(P,D,Q,s)

where:

p,d,q → non-seasonal components
P,D,Q → seasonal components
s → seasonal period

For monthly data with yearly seasonality:

s = 12
37. Machine Learning for Time Series

You can also convert a time series into a supervised ML problem.

Suppose:

Day    Sales
1      100
2      120
3      130
4      150
5      160

Create lag features:

Day    Lag1    Lag2    Sales
3      120     100     130
4      130     120     150
5      150     130     160

Now the problem becomes:

X = [Lag1, Lag2]
y = Sales

Then you can use ML models.

38. Feature Engineering for Time Series

This is extremely important when using ML.

Useful features include:

Lag features
df['lag_1'] = df['Sales'].shift(1)
df['lag_7'] = df['Sales'].shift(7)
df['lag_30'] = df['Sales'].shift(30)
Rolling features
df['rolling_7'] = df['Sales'].rolling(7).mean()
Date features
df['month'] = df.index.month
df['day'] = df.index.day
df['day_of_week'] = df.index.dayofweek

You can also create:

weekend indicator
holiday indicator
quarter
week number
year
days since start
seasonal indicators
39. Forecast Horizon

The forecast horizon means how far into the future you're predicting.

Examples:

One-step forecasting

Predict:

Tomorrow
Multi-step forecasting

Predict:

Next 7 days

or:

Next 12 months

The farther into the future you forecast, the more uncertainty generally accumulates.

40. One-Step vs Multi-Step

Suppose you have:

Jan
Feb
Mar

and want to predict:

Apr
May
Jun
One-step

Predict April using known historical data.

Then potentially use the newly predicted April value when forecasting May.

Direct multi-step

Build separate predictions for:

April
May
June

There are multiple strategies for multi-step forecasting.

41. Forecast Evaluation

After making predictions, you need to evaluate them.

Common metrics include:

MAE

Mean Absolute Error

MAE = average(|actual - predicted|)

Easy to interpret.

If:

MAE = 10

the average absolute prediction error is 10 units.

MSE

Mean Squared Error

MSE = average((actual - predicted)²)

Large errors are penalized more heavily.

RMSE

Root Mean Squared Error

RMSE = √MSE

It has the same units as the original target variable.

MAPE

Mean Absolute Percentage Error

Measures error in percentage terms.

However, MAPE can behave badly when actual values are zero or very close to zero.

42. Prediction Intervals

A forecast isn't always just:

Tomorrow = 500

You can also have uncertainty around the prediction.

For example:

Forecast = 500

Prediction interval:
450 – 550

This communicates that the exact future value is uncertain.

43. Data Leakage in Time Series

This is one of the biggest mistakes beginners make.

Suppose you're predicting:

January 2025

You must not accidentally use information from:

February 2025

during training.

For example, calculating a feature using future observations can leak information.

The fundamental rule is:

When predicting time T, don't use information that would not have been available at time T.

44. Missing Values

Time series can contain missing dates or missing observations.

Example:

Jan 1 → 100
Jan 2 → 120
Jan 3 → NaN
Jan 4 → 140

Possible approaches include:

forward fill
backward fill
interpolation
domain-specific imputation

For example:

df['Sales'] = df['Sales'].ffill()

But the appropriate method depends on the data and what the missing value represents.

45. Outliers

Time series can contain unusual observations.

Example:

100
105
102
110
500
108

500 might be an outlier.

But be careful:

An unusual value isn't automatically an error.

It could represent a genuine event such as:

holiday
promotion
pandemic
system failure
unusual weather
major event

So don't automatically remove every outlier.

46. Seasonality vs Trend

This distinction is very important.

Trend

Long-term direction:

↗ ↗ ↗ ↗
Seasonality

Repeating pattern:

↗ ↘ ↗ ↘ ↗ ↘

Example:

A company's sales might be increasing every year and have higher sales every December.

Then you have:

Trend + Seasonality
