49. Time Series in Machine Learning

There are two broad ways you will encounter time series.

Traditional statistical forecasting

Models directly designed around temporal structure:

AR
MA
ARMA
ARIMA
SARIMA
Exponential Smoothing
Holt-Winters
Machine Learning approach

Convert the time series into features:

Lag features
Rolling statistics
Calendar features
External variables

Then use models such as:

Linear Regression
Random Forest
XGBoost
LightGBM
Neural Networks

Neither approach is automatically appropriate for every dataset. The choice depends on the structure of the data, forecasting objective, available features, and evaluation setup.

50. Time Series vs Regression

This is a common interview question.

Regression

You might have:

X → Age, Salary, Experience
y → House Price

Rows don't necessarily have a temporal order.

Time Series

You have:

Past → Present → Future

The ordering is essential.

The model must respect the temporal structure.

51. Most Important Concepts to Remember

If you're studying this for ML, make sure you understand these particularly well:

Concept	Meaning
Time Series	Data observed over time
Trend	Long-term direction
Seasonality	Repeating pattern
Cycle	Long-term non-fixed fluctuation
Noise	Random variation
Lag	Previous observation
Rolling Mean	Average over a moving window
Autocorrelation	Relationship with past values
ACF	Correlation at different lags
PACF	Direct correlation at different lags
Stationarity	Stable statistical behavior over time
Differencing	Subtracting previous values
Forecasting	Predicting future values
Forecast Horizon	How far into the future we predict
Data Leakage	Using unavailable future information
TimeSeriesSplit	Time-aware cross-validation
